# TODO

## General

- Consider using a centralized logging solution like ELK or Datadog or loki with Grafana for better observability.

## New Features

- Projects / Workspaces: group related conversations, files, agents, tools, preferences, and instructions into persistent workspaces for long-running work.
- Deep Research mode: run longer multi-step research workflows with source citations, confidence notes, step traces, and exportable reports.
- Scheduled Tasks: let users create one-off or recurring agent jobs that run later, complete while the user is offline, and notify them with results.
- Artifacts / Canvas: add an editable side workspace for generated reports, markdown docs, code, tables, diagrams, JSON configs, and other reusable outputs.
- Agent run timeline rebuilt around a claude.ai-style interleaved chain-of-thought + content pattern. The AI message body becomes a temporal sequence — collapsible "Thinking" blocks that expand into the model's CoT + tool executions and always end with a "Done" sentinel, alternating with content blocks (`[CoT, Content, CoT, Content, …]`) so the structure mirrors how the model actually worked. Live sub-agent panels stay inline ONLY during streaming; once the run terminates, the plan card and sub-agent panels collapse out of the message body into two buttons on the AI action bar — a Plan button that opens a side panel with the full plan, and a Sub-agents button with a top-right badge showing the delegated count that opens a side panel with all sub-agent activity. Persistence is handled by replaying the raw event log (already the source of truth via `useInferenceRuns` + `runsByConversation`) rather than a new message field — storing a pre-rendered timeline alongside the raw events creates drift risk every time the renderer changes, which it will. Derive the timeline structure once on hydration via `useMemo` keyed by the run's terminal event id so we don't re-walk the events on every render but also don't commit a stored shape to the DB. The "Done" sentinel must only fire when the run reaches a terminal status (success / error / cancelled), not when the last `THINKING_END` event arrives — otherwise a paused HITL run looks complete when it isn't. This is also the surface that owns HITL approval cards inline within the CoT block (use [HITL Event](https://ai-sdk.dev/elements/components/confirmation) or a custom one), and individual tool calls within the Thinking block should render with the [actions](https://elements.ai-sdk.dev/components/task) or [tool](https://elements.ai-sdk.dev/components/tool) primitives from ai-sdk so users can see what was called, what came back, and how long it took. The HITL approval panel itself also needs a redesign — today it appears as a card in the center of the chat which shoves content around and pulls focus away from where the user expects interaction; replace it with a compact surface that takes over the input bar position when an interrupt is pending, so the action lives at the bottom where the user is already looking and the chat scroll stays anchored to the latest message.
- DB-backed skill registry with per-user pool and per-agent assignments. Move skills from filesystem-as-truth to DB-as-truth so deletes cascade instead of leaving orphan copies on disk. One global `skills` table is the source of truth for every skill that exists; users add skills from it to their own personal registry, then assign skills from their registry to specific deep agents — tracked as `(user_id, agent_id, skill_id)`. Users can search the global registry to add more skills, and can create their own skills (the create flow is the complex part — needs a form, content validation, ownership/visibility, and edit/delete). The on-disk `<user>/agents/<slug>/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` becomes a cache refreshed on assign; cascade deletes wipe the matching directories so removing a skill anywhere up the chain cleans everything below.

## Agents

- Update the retrieval process and the whole RAG pipelines so that it will be like an mcp tool calling.
- Add end-to-end file attachment support in inference, routed through the per-conversation filesystem. Bridge: persist uploaded files to `<user>/agents/<slug>/<conv-id>/input/<filename>` (in addition to / instead of the current Postgres blob path). Deep agents: split `/conversation/` into `input/` (read-only — user uploads) and `output/` (read-write — agent-generated artifacts), add a read-only backend wrapper for `input/` since `deepagents==0.4.11` lacks `FilesystemPermission`, and update Omni's system prompt to direct writes to `/conversation/output/`. LangGraph agents: normalize/parse file parts into the agent state (these agents have no filesystem mount).
- Lock down the per-(user, agent, conversation) workspace so the running agent can never alter its own permissions or mount layout. Today this is implicit (deepagents' filesystem tools expose only ls/read/write/edit/glob/grep — no chmod/chown/mount) but becomes load-bearing the moment we add an execute tool. At that point we also need to resolve the conflict between sandbox isolation and persistent file storage: an ephemeral sandbox per call gives full isolation but loses access to `/conversation/input/` and `/conversation/output/`; mounting the workspace into the sandbox preserves file flow but the sandbox's isolation is then scoped to "everything outside the workspace." Pick a path before shipping execute — likely a gVisor/Firecracker/Daytona-style kernel with `input/` bind-mounted read-only, `output/` read-write, no network by default, dropped caps, no-new-privileges, and hard CPU+memory+time limits.

## Dialogue Bridge

- Add the pgvector in order to have an embedding for each conversation and then we can use this embedding to find the most relevant conversations for a given query, this will be useful for the retrieval process and memory across chats.

## Agentic UI

- Chart can be visualized with [shadcn/charts](https://www.shadcn.io/charts) and the agent can have a custom tool like the todo tool in order to represent the chart and create a custom AGUI event for the interaction with the chart.
